Throughout this passage and throughout wider American literature the conflict between traditional lifestyles and modernity is vital and frequently explored. This is shown in other novels I have studied such as *The Grapes of Wrath.* In the Grapes of Wrath the desire for a traditional agrarian lifestyle is represented by characters such as Ma Joad whereas characters such as Connie and Al Joad desire modernity. Similarly, in this passage Henry desires modernity and change whereas his mother seeks to maintain the life they have always had. Henry views war as distinctly European ('heavy crowns and high castles') and something of the past. In this way Henry seeks to form a new identity for himself using neo-classical ideas to make himself into a 'great man' which can be seen through his mention of the battles being 'Homeric' or 'Greeklike'. This can relate to another novel I read *The Great Gatsby* where similarly Gatsby leaves his family in search of greatness in the form of wealth and success. This ambitious abandonment of modern life is consistent throughout American literature and can correlate to the American Dream. The American Dream is quite individualised and Henry's dreams ('dreamed' 'visions') are based on war. American identity being linked to war is a commonplace idea the national holiday of America celebrates battle victory and veteran culture is incredibly important to this day, whilst also having the most powerful military in Henry's time. Thus, it only makes sense that when trying to forge a new modern life and identity Henry looks to war and conflict something he uniquely views as in the past. Despite seeing war as the past Henry sees it as his future and his path into a modern life away from his families traditions.

